  "elements": [
    {
      "id": "4QiSTmrbhyYcXqkqTE4yR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1102.4572461962528,
      "y": 3026.1448860209202,
      "width": 1602.21875,
      "height": 1475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b68",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1239840546,
      "version": 3575,
      "versionNonce": 962440290,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781696283681,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "HASHING:\n\n-> Take a key, convert it to a number by using a hash function.\n-> Store the value at the index of result of the hash function.\n-> It is possible that the hash function will map two keys onto the same index, when the elements in the hash table (n) is greater than slots in the table (m)\n-> The above phenomenon is called \"Collision\"\n\nCollision Resolution Techniques:\n\n-> Chaining (Closed Addressing)\n-> Linear Probing\n-> Quadratic Probing     Open Addressing\n->  Double Hashing\n\n\nChaining:\n-> If two keys map to the same slot, we store the pair (key, value) in a chain of linked list nodes.\n-> Load Factor(α) = n/m\n-> For chaining, The worst case time for searching is O(n)\n-> The average case for for searching is O(1 + α).\n\nOpen Addressing:\n-> In Open Addressing, if there is a collision, we search for another free slot to put the (key, value) pair in the hash table.\n-> We do this by the hash function slot = h(key, i) = (h'(key) + f(i)) % size, where i is the \"Trial Count\" (the i th try for finding a slot).\n\nLinear Probing:\n-> We probe(test) for slot linearly, i.e check the next slot and so on. => f(i) = i\n-> Insert: get the slot by using the hash function, if it is occupied, check the next slot, insert the entry in the first encountered free slot.\n-> Search: check the slots linearly, until you encounter a free slot. \n-> Delete: you cannot directly \"free\" a slot, as search depends on the linear flow of occupied slots. so, we just mark that the slot is deleted and \n    available for a new entry.\n\n-> Major disadvantage is clusters tend form in a linear fashion starting from a single slot. These are called \"Primary Clusters\"\n-> So, any key that hashes nearby this cluster, will have to go through a mini linear search to find the key or to find a free slot.\n-> This makes the worst case time complexity to be O(n)\n\n\nQuadratic Probing:\n-> f(i) = c1 * i + c2 * i^2\n-> This effectively breaks primary clustering as the hash function skips a portion of array rather than growing sequentially.\n-> But still if \"n\" keys map to same hash value, then we still need to go through n non-sequential slots sequentially to find next free spot.\n-> This is called \"Secondary Clustering\".\n\n-> Disadvantages are : It has worse spatial cache locality than Linear Probing.\n-> If not carefully configured, (hash table size a prime number and load factor less than 50%), it will fail to find an empty slot, even when the table isn't full.\n-> This still has same kind of sequential search problem as linear probing, if a key's initial hash matches with some of the entries in the table. \n\nDouble Hashing:\n-> f(i) = i * h2(key)\n-> This solves secondary Clustering. But it is still possible to form some kind of clusters, when both h1 and h2 gives same result for multiple keys. But this is rare.\n\nPossible Probe Sequences:\n-> Linear : m\n-> Quadratic : m\n-> Double hashing: m^2\n-> Uniform Hashing: m!\n\n-> As long as we keep the table from getting completely full (usually by resizing/rehashing when α > 0.7), \n    the average number of probes required for a search remains a constant.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "HASHING:\n\n-> Take a key, convert it to a number by using a hash function.\n-> Store the value at the index of result of the hash function.\n-> It is possible that the hash function will map two keys onto the same index, when the elements in the hash table (n) is greater than slots in the table (m)\n-> The above phenomenon is called \"Collision\"\n\nCollision Resolution Techniques:\n\n-> Chaining (Closed Addressing)\n-> Linear Probing\n-> Quadratic Probing     Open Addressing\n->  Double Hashing\n\n\nChaining:\n-> If two keys map to the same slot, we store the pair (key, value) in a chain of linked list nodes.\n-> Load Factor(α) = n/m\n-> For chaining, The worst case time for searching is O(n)\n-> The average case for for searching is O(1 + α).\n\nOpen Addressing:\n-> In Open Addressing, if there is a collision, we search for another free slot to put the (key, value) pair in the hash table.\n-> We do this by the hash function slot = h(key, i) = (h'(key) + f(i)) % size, where i is the \"Trial Count\" (the i th try for finding a slot).\n\nLinear Probing:\n-> We probe(test) for slot linearly, i.e check the next slot and so on. => f(i) = i\n-> Insert: get the slot by using the hash function, if it is occupied, check the next slot, insert the entry in the first encountered free slot.\n-> Search: check the slots linearly, until you encounter a free slot. \n-> Delete: you cannot directly \"free\" a slot, as search depends on the linear flow of occupied slots. so, we just mark that the slot is deleted and \n    available for a new entry.\n\n-> Major disadvantage is clusters tend form in a linear fashion starting from a single slot. These are called \"Primary Clusters\"\n-> So, any key that hashes nearby this cluster, will have to go through a mini linear search to find the key or to find a free slot.\n-> This makes the worst case time complexity to be O(n)\n\n\nQuadratic Probing:\n-> f(i) = c1 * i + c2 * i^2\n-> This effectively breaks primary clustering as the hash function skips a portion of array rather than growing sequentially.\n-> But still if \"n\" keys map to same hash value, then we still need to go through n non-sequential slots sequentially to find next free spot.\n-> This is called \"Secondary Clustering\".\n\n-> Disadvantages are : It has worse spatial cache locality than Linear Probing.\n-> If not carefully configured, (hash table size a prime number and load factor less than 50%), it will fail to find an empty slot, even when the table isn't full.\n-> This still has same kind of sequential search problem as linear probing, if a key's initial hash matches with some of the entries in the table. \n\nDouble Hashing:\n-> f(i) = i * h2(key)\n-> This solves secondary Clustering. But it is still possible to form some kind of clusters, when both h1 and h2 gives same result for multiple keys. But this is rare.\n\nPossible Probe Sequences:\n-> Linear : m\n-> Quadratic : m\n-> Double hashing: m^2\n-> Uniform Hashing: m!\n\n-> As long as we keep the table from getting completely full (usually by resizing/rehashing when α > 0.7), \n    the average number of probes required for a search remains a constant.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
