    {
      "id": "sSl9Kf5jiae0B2E--9ybP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1145.4664605810967,
      "y": 6027.435961114313,
      "width": 1433.158447265625,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 245136304,
      "version": 248,
      "versionNonce": 995089840,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781750843151,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> How to detect Path/Pattern of Imbalance?\n    - Always go to the direction, whose height is greater. \n    - In LL: Direction of y is greater than a and direction of z is greater than b. (Shown Above.)\n    - Then, based on the pattern, the subtrees of the node which gets to become the \"center\" of the new pattern, shall be connected accordingly.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> How to detect Path/Pattern of Imbalance?\n    - Always go to the direction, whose height is greater. \n    - In LL: Direction of y is greater than a and direction of z is greater than b. (Shown Above.)\n    - Then, based on the pattern, the subtrees of the node which gets to become the \"center\" of the new pattern, shall be connected accordingly.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "D3gdriWMlKvV_6H2rStxP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1145.878593449773,
      "y": 6219.338482169756,
      "width": 1527.0787353515625,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1165827504,
      "version": 1653,
      "versionNonce": 1175196592,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781751894578,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Deletion of a node in AVL Tree:\n\n-> If the node is an \"inner\" node. Then you have to choose a replacement for it from two of it's children.\n-> If both of their heights are same, Then it doesn't matter which one you choose.\n-> Otherwise, always choose the one with greater height, to reduce the chances of imbalance further up the tree.\n\n-> What if the deleted node is a leaf node?\n-> We traverse up to root and check for imbalances. We use the same procedure described above to find the pattern and fix the imbalance.\n-> But after deletion we might encounter a case where, At x, there is a clear imbalance and after we go a level deep to find out the next direction\n    the heights are same in both directions.\n-> In such cases, always choose the \"Single Rotation\" patterns, (LL and RR) because of code performance reasons. In normal cases, always choose the side\n    with greater height.\n\n-> In Insertion, we can stop the traversal to root after fixing am imbalance.\n-> But, In deletion, we shall continue till the root no matter what. This is because:\n    - Fixing an imbalance in a sub tree, might shrink it's height which could cause an imbalance at the higher level nodes.\n\n-> Maximum number of nodes in a AVL tree given it's height \"h\" => 2^(h+1) - 1\n-> Minimum number of nodes in a AVL tree given it's height \"h\" => N(h) = N(h-1) + N(h-2) + 1",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Deletion of a node in AVL Tree:\n\n-> If the node is an \"inner\" node. Then you have to choose a replacement for it from two of it's children.\n-> If both of their heights are same, Then it doesn't matter which one you choose.\n-> Otherwise, always choose the one with greater height, to reduce the chances of imbalance further up the tree.\n\n-> What if the deleted node is a leaf node?\n-> We traverse up to root and check for imbalances. We use the same procedure described above to find the pattern and fix the imbalance.\n-> But after deletion we might encounter a case where, At x, there is a clear imbalance and after we go a level deep to find out the next direction\n    the heights are same in both directions.\n-> In such cases, always choose the \"Single Rotation\" patterns, (LL and RR) because of code performance reasons. In normal cases, always choose the side\n    with greater height.\n\n-> In Insertion, we can stop the traversal to root after fixing am imbalance.\n-> But, In deletion, we shall continue till the root no matter what. This is because:\n    - Fixing an imbalance in a sub tree, might shrink it's height which could cause an imbalance at the higher level nodes.\n\n-> Maximum number of nodes in a AVL tree given it's height \"h\" => 2^(h+1) - 1\n-> Minimum number of nodes in a AVL tree given it's height \"h\" => N(h) = N(h-1) + N(h-2) + 1",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
